<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Purana Qila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1530s–1540s</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Old Fort, Delhi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Purana Qila, the Old Fort, is a fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> built on a site traditionally identified with Indraprastha. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> began a city called Dinpanah there in the 1530s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> rebuilt and completed the fort, adding the Qila-i-Kuhna mosque. The Sher Mandal pavilion inside was used by Humayun as a library, and he died after falling on its stairs in 1556.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/purana-qila.jpg" alt="Purana Qila" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Airknight at English Wikipedia · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purana_Qila_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Excavations by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> under B. B. Lal in 1954–55 and 1969–72 found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware</a> dating to about 1000 BCE, and later finds show habitation through the Mauryan, Shunga, Kushana, Gupta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and Mughal periods. Alexander Cunningham identified the site with Indraprastha, and into the early 20th century it was known as Pandavon Ka Qila, Pandava's fort. Matching such literary traditions to archaeological remains is, however, almost always methodologically impossible.</p><p>The fort began as the walls of Dinpanah, the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> that the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> was building. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a>, strengthened its fortifications, completed its walls and called his fort Shergarh. How much each ruler built is disputed, as the primary sources differ: Abbas Sarwani says both forts were unfinished at Sher Shah's death, and Tarikh-i-Khan-Jahan credits Salim Shah Suri with a wall defending Dinpanah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 7 October 1556 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hemu">Hem Chandra Vikramaditya</a>, who had defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s forces at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, was crowned in the fort; a month later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-panipat">Second Battle of Panipat</a>. In the 1920s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens">Edwin Lutyens</a> aligned the central vista of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> with it. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">Partition of India</a> in 1947 it held a camp for Muslims bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, with between 150,000 and 200,000 refugees by September; the camp ran until early 1948.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the British interned Japanese civilians there: by December 1942 the Purana Quila camp held 2,115, most from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, in tents that gave little protection from winter cold or summer heat. From the 1970s its ramparts became a setting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, beginning with three productions of the National School of Drama directed by Ebrahim Alkazi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The walls rise to 18 metres and run for about 1.5 km, with three double-storeyed sandstone gateways: the Bara Darwaza facing west, still in use, the south or Humayun Gate, and the Talaqi Gate. Each is flanked by semicircular bastions and decorated with marble inlay, blue tiles, jharokhas and chhatris. Of the buildings inside, little survives besides the Sher Mandal and the single-domed Qila-i-Kuhna Mosque of 1541, a Friday mosque with five horseshoe arches and the best preserved building of the fort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Purana Qila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sher Shah reorganised revenue on measured land, introduced the silver rupiya that gave the rupee its name, built the Grand Trunk Road from Bengal to the Punjab with caravanserais and a postal service, and built the Rohtas Fort and the <b>Purana Qila</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a></li></ul></section>
